1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:14,860
I'm Yoko Hikasa. I'm Ayane Sakura. We've started! Wait—you're filming my phone? I'm going to show it.

2
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:27,920
We're filming our phones. This is also broadcast on terrestrial radio, and then slightly later on Bunka Broadcasting's online video service, Cho! A&G+.

3
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:42,320
By the way, Cho! A&G+ has famously terrible picture quality. Has it improved during the six months I was away? You're insulting them immediately!

4
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:47,660
The A&G+ picture quality hasn't improved. Maybe it is slightly better?

5
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,640
Isn't this the quality viewers receive?

6
00:00:50,700 --> 00:01:05,580
No. We're looking at ourselves on a 4K monitor, but the feed goes through something like a two-kilobit garbage encode.

7
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:18,780
They should start over from the Heisei era. The feed gets horror-film noise and artifacts—seriously. Then why did they put this 4K television in the studio?

8
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:32,540
There is no point! But we're being filmed with 4K cameras, so the 4K source exists somewhere inside Bunka Broadcasting.

9
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:47,260
That is pointless—cut the budget. Bunka Broadcasting uses the low-quality stream as an excuse not to provide hair and makeup, yet still broadcasts video. Don't waste money!

10
00:01:47,260 --> 00:02:05,560
What are these cameras and monitors for? Perhaps their full power will appear if we release a DVD or do some other fundraising project, and then we'll receive hair and makeup...

11
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:19,840
...so they can sell the footage and fund the show. What are we saying in the very first episode? We need to explain what kind of company Bunka Broadcasting is.

12
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:34,860
Hiyo-san hasn't been here in a while either. I have never had a key to enter Bunka Broadcasting—a card key, I mean. You have visited for anime-related radio shows, though.

13
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:52,700
I came occasionally for those. But now I just enter by recognition. Isn't Bunka Broadcasting's security rather lax? Takada Junji entered by recognition too. Once you're Takada Junji or Yoko Hikasa, they recognize you.

14
00:02:52,700 --> 00:03:05,700
It's Yoko Hikasa, by the way. For my honor, I should mention that they recognized me too. Look at you! The security guard was someone I knew.

15
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:21,980
I said “Good morning” and walked in confidently. Could an ordinary person do that too? Absolutely not—do not imitate us.

16
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:36,740
The show is also distributed later as audio through the internet radio station Onsen, operated by Tablier Communications. Since this is episode one, we should explain that. When video is present...

17
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:51,760
...you naturally keep referring to things viewers can see. From about episode two, we'll stop paying attention to the camera. You can decide that for yourself, but you don't control how I feel!

18
00:03:51,760 --> 00:04:05,140
We will stop noticing it. Understood? By episode two, Yoko Hikasa will stop noticing this 4K camera.

19
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:18,680
Why are you deciding for me? Treat it like a fixed security camera—you don't deliberately look into a security camera. I've been looking directly into it quite a lot. So have I; the habit hasn't worn off.

20
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:35,280
Still, this is fundamentally an audio program. Since it airs on terrestrial radio, taxi and truck drivers or people working late at night may happen to hear it.

21
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:49,500
Exactly. Won't they think, “These two are noisy,” and switch to TBS Radio? Then we'd be lowering Bunka Broadcasting's value. We have to work out how not to do that.

22
00:04:49,500 --> 00:05:02,700
Starting before thinking about that is already strange. We need to work out how to retain viewers—listeners—and persuade them to hear all our digressions.

23
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:12,120
This is a program where we let you hear that planning meeting. Will the meeting last the whole run—perhaps a year?

24
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:27,760
People will yell, “Decide already!” Incidentally, the show technically hasn't begun until we say the title. I have an idea for keeping unfortunate terrestrial listeners who don't know voice actors listening.

25
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:41,440
We should speak calmly in lower voices. Lower voices?

26
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:48,940
It airs at one in the morning. Right—no radio show delivers this much energy at 1 a.m.

27
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:55,620
We're loud. Are you aiming for a calm program, Saku?

28
00:05:55,840 --> 00:06:09,360
Yes. Then they chose the wrong hosts—and the wrong staff. Even the music is wrong.

29
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:23,800
Saying that ruins everything! We became voice actors because we wanted a Bunka Broadcasting radio show. Sorry, I didn't. Why won't you play along once? I became one because I wanted to act.

30
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:37,400
How admirable. Shouldn't we introduce ourselves for listeners who don't know us? What should we do—an octopus introduction?

31
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:52,160
An octopus introduction? `Jiko` means oneself! This only works on video and not at all in audio, and now you're impersonating an octopus. Yoko Hikasa was born in the Showa era.

32
00:06:52,160 --> 00:07:07,380
She is a Showa-born voice actor. She frequently plays characters with large breasts and has a late-night kind of voice.

33
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:23,880
A late-night voice—more than me. She has almost no child-oriented roles. But despite appearances, she is also excellent at gentle, embracing mothers and older sisters. What do you mean, “despite appearances”?

34
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:38,020
And that concludes it! Could you say something more personal? Right, because this is radio. As a person, she is...

35
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:41,420
...someone who laughs very easily.

36
00:07:45,540 --> 00:08:02,780
We're both big laughers. We often go out for meals, and originally we were senior and junior at the same agency, different from our current affiliations.

37
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:16,520
That's right. She looked after me enormously. We've known each other for a very long time.

38
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:33,680
I've known Saku since she was fifteen or sixteen. I almost asked how old she is now, but realized the question would rebound onto me.

39
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:47,920
Twenty-nine? Stop! I'm twenty-four! Enough! You're thirty-something, right?

40
00:08:47,920 --> 00:09:07,100
Once you pass thirty-three, you lose track. Some people mysteriously conceal their age, while others resist that custom by announcing theirs. Which are you? Perhaps I'll hide mine.

41
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:25,060
I don't intentionally hide it. At a ceremonial first pitch, everyone else had an age listed, while I was described as “Yoko Hikasa, age undisclosed.” I never said it was private.

42
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:44,340
The writer probably assumed it was forbidden. Coincidentally, the same happened when I appeared in a newspaper: other people had ages in parentheses, while ours were blank.

43
00:09:44,340 --> 00:09:58,760
Apparently, outlets omit it when an agency profile lists only the birthday and not the birth year. Our former agency did exactly that.

44
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:12,900
That explains how they treated us. It wasn't because Hiyo-san refused to state her age. What a relief—we solved a mystery that had bothered us for years.

45
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:26,180
Now I belong to the long-established Aoni Production, but my age still isn't listed. So it becomes “undisclosed” again. For some reason...

46
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:31,220
...they are protecting something, even though I'm still in my twenties. What?

47
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:46,340
The profile is extremely simple: qualifications, birthday, birthplace, licenses, hobbies—“wearing clothes.” “Wearing clothes” is your hobby? Would someone without that hobby go naked?

48
00:10:46,340 --> 00:11:00,120
Stop nitpicking! What else was I supposed to write? You mean fashion. What are Hiyo-san's current hobbies?

49
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:18,100
Hiyo-san is freelance and has no official website. What happened to her blog, `Hiyokko-ki`? It is gone. That began as a Livedoor blog, didn't it? Then it moved to LINE Blog...

50
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:32,580
...and now I use Ameba Blog. Everyone, please look at Yoko Hikasa's blog. There is a mysterious woman sitting with her legs folded to one side.

51
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:48,960
Don't stare! You look like the Little Mermaid after she grew legs—that sort of pose.

52
00:11:48,960 --> 00:12:05,380
The photo correction worked wonderfully. No, this is reality! When LINE Blog was discontinued, I moved to Ameba and also use Instagram.

53
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:21,380
LINE Blog preserved my old posts after the service transition, apparently.

54
00:12:21,380 --> 00:12:36,820
Since you're freelance, what happens when the press needs your age? They might check Wikipedia or ask you directly. Shall we disclose it on this program?

55
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:50,100
“Thirty-eight.” Stop that! It's already on Wikipedia. But Wikipedia is written by ordinary users and could contain incorrect information.

56
00:12:50,100 --> 00:13:06,440
Exactly—I might actually be forty-eight. What do you want, Hiyo-san? I don't know anymore. I don't know where I'm running either.

57
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:27,100
May I use this program to say one thing? Please remove my Wikipedia photo. Yes, please. I'm standing in front of far too many bright red roses. Can people upload that image without permission?

58
00:13:27,100 --> 00:13:45,720
They shouldn't. What about portrait rights? Those rights belong to Aoni Production now. I don't know why that photo was used; it was from some film festival, and several people have photos with that rose background.

59
00:13:45,720 --> 00:14:01,560
I've said this many times, but my eyes are half closed in it. That's why I want it removed. It was bright outside. I see. Still, you look beautiful and young.

60
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:14,680
I genuinely want it gone. Of course I look young—the photo is already five years old. Wikipedia photos should be kept current, perhaps updated once a year. Or just use my official profile photo.

61
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:27,700
Now you've said it, people may use it. Honestly, it is much better than the roses. Even if Aoni Production refuses, I personally give permission: replace it with my official agency publicity photo.

62
00:14:27,700 --> 00:14:30,420
Understood. Who is supposed to do that—me?

63
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:43,680
Wikipedia! Anyway, we wandered off topic. Hiyo-san and I have known each other for years. Where did this story begin? When I was sixteen.

64
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:57,460
We've known each other since then, and she still takes me out for meals. Still—`nau`? That's obsolete slang; don't use it.

65
00:14:57,460 --> 00:14:57,660
`Nau`?

66
00:14:58,780 --> 00:14:59,660
Is it obsolete?

67
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:03,700
Probably. You aren't exactly keeping up with the times.

68
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:06,840
Are you all right?

69
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:15,180
You're in no position to claim you're twenty-four or twenty-five. `Was`! Even `was` is old. Do you understand `was`?

70
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:18,020
The past. Wait, what was `was` again?

71
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:19,620
What?

72
00:15:20,760 --> 00:15:21,200
Did I say it?

73
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:34,580
For example, “I went”—it already passed. The current era is the past! Wait, let's correct our course.

74
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:48,440
Impressive. Anyway, she often takes me to dinner. We haven't progressed at all—how many minutes have elapsed without a title call? It is episode one; we have things to explain.

75
00:15:48,440 --> 00:16:01,940
We still go to dinner together, and whenever we talk we spend the whole time laughing. This is exactly what happens: we wander away from the subject.

76
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:16,160
We take detours, laugh loudly, and eventually pause and ask, “What were we talking about?” Once we had dinner with Nao Toyama, and our conversational tempo was too fast for her.

77
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:32,540
Gradually, Nao began fading out of the conversation. It was cute but also pitiful.

78
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:51,940
We need to tell the Akari Kitō story too, so let's hurry. At Animelo Summer Live in August...

79
00:16:51,940 --> 00:17:08,420
Akari Kitō was appearing with Love Live! Nijigasaki immediately before our performance.

80
00:17:08,420 --> 00:17:17,620
She repeatedly rose on a stage lift doing a peculiar piece of choreography. You're intensely conscious of the video again!

81
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:31,700
Don't mock other people! It was a cute, strange, slightly goofy dance. “Akari Kitō performed a mysterious dance.”

82
00:17:31,700 --> 00:17:50,620
She did it about five times, so I memorized it and teased her by imitating it. We're close friends. On the day of Animelo Summer Live...

83
00:17:50,620 --> 00:18:07,440
...a staff member sent me several photos taken from the backstage monitor during our performance. I thanked the staff member.

84
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:16,960
Later they wrote, “You were gone when I noticed. I wanted to take a photo together.” Together?

85
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:32,060
Yes. I wondered why, since they were a staff member I'd met only once and had barely messaged.

86
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:46,920
Our exchanges had only been formal pleasantries such as “Happy birthday” and “Thank you.” Why were they suddenly being so familiar?

87
00:18:46,920 --> 00:19:01,000
Then I learned that Akari had recently been consulting people about it. She was messaging Saku about doing this new show with Hiyo-san...

88
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,260
...over LINE. You're discussing that on LINE?

89
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:19,500
Akari said that since she'd enjoyed seeing Hiyo-san at Animelo Summer Live, she had sent Hiyo-san lots of monitor photos.

90
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:35,320
She had been too busy to take a photo together, so she wrote that she wished they had. Hikasa replied incredibly curtly. “Does Hikasa-san become a different person on LINE?”

91
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:57,020
That was what she was asking. Akari is sensitive, so apparently she consulted several people, including Junya Enoki and others.

92
00:19:58,820 --> 00:20:13,560
Saku said, “That did sound cold. She's right in front of me, so I'll ask her.” We happened to be recording promotional material for this show.

93
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:32,200
When Sakura showed Hikasa the messages, everything finally clicked: Hikasa had mistaken Akari for a staff member because their LINE display names were identical.

94
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:46,600
Hikasa had never changed Akari's contact name, so the unexpected birthday messages and familiarity had confused her. It was years ago, and she had met the staff member only once, so her memory was vague.

95
00:20:46,600 --> 00:21:00,240
After realizing the mistake, Hikasa said she owed Akari an apology meal: “I'll treat you to anything you want.”

96
00:21:00,240 --> 00:21:13,220
Akari replied, “If I can go with Hiyo-san, I'd eat anything—even bean sprouts.” So Hikasa decided bean sprouts were enough. She didn't say she specifically wanted bean sprouts! I got carried away.

97
00:21:13,720 --> 00:21:26,500
When Akari first moved to Tokyo and had no money, she survived on bean sprouts. That's sad—please buy her something better. She probably likes them! Even so, buy her something better.

98
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:46,860
Akari, come join us. That is the slightly awkward relationship between Akari Kitō and Yoko Hikasa. I'm genuinely glad we cleared it up before the show began; for the first time, I thought doing this radio show was worthwhile.

99
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:59,040
So, I'm Yoko Hikasa. Did that communicate what kind of person I am? Now introduce me. Right—Saku-chan is...

100
00:21:59,980 --> 00:22:12,980
Well, she entered this industry around fifteen and is a sensitive person. Isn't that description rather thin?

101
00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:28,980
Very thin. I guess I don't know anything about Saku. But although she was sensitive when we met, she always had an exceptionally quick mind.

102
00:22:28,980 --> 00:22:42,500
That was her greatest weapon. Because she's smart, she absorbed all kinds of things and developed remarkably fast. She could be a little shy around people,

103
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:57,500
and sometimes timid, but she has fully blossomed. She's a great communicator now, with acquaintances not only in voice acting but throughout all kinds of industries.

104
00:22:57,500 --> 00:23:03,740
Sometimes I wonder what realm she is the emperor of. Isn't that going a little too far?

105
00:23:03,740 --> 00:23:17,700
“Jungle Emperor Saku.” That wasn't even clever—the vowels don't match, the reference is old, and will kids in the Reiwa era even understand it? You don't know Jungle Emperor Leo?

106
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:32,220
I'm about the youngest who would. I only know it because my mother told me about it. Then what do you know? Death Note? Death Note probably resonates with people in their thirties.

107
00:23:32,220 --> 00:23:47,660
People in their twenties too. What would resonate with teenagers listening now? Muzan? Oh—Muzan!

108
00:23:47,660 --> 00:24:01,900
The boss of the enemies. “So you were the enemy!” Now I'm suddenly being cast as a villain while you get excited all by yourself.

109
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:15,900
You called yourself an emperor, so I thought of Toshiyuki Morikawa. That's the BL Emperor! Hey, don't go there. Anyway, for our first episode—

110
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:40,120
let's do the title call: “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions.” This talk-variety show features two people who, left alone, could talk for hours,

111
00:24:40,120 --> 00:24:50,860
but makes them finish in exactly thirty minutes without allowing digressions. The show has an official X account too. Please follow it and liven things up with a hashtag—but shall we decide the hashtag next week?

112
00:24:50,860 --> 00:24:52,860
Then what are people supposed to use this time?

113
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:54,860
Wait, we're already—what?

114
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:58,860
We're twenty-something minutes in already, surely. Exactly, so what do we do?

115
00:24:58,860 --> 00:25:07,860
Anything is fine; just choose one. I don't know—staff will probably decide it and announce it on X beforehand. It'll probably just be #余談 or something.

116
00:25:07,860 --> 00:25:15,860
Maybe #ヨダユル, #ヒカサクラ, or #余談許さない. “Yodayuru” sounds lame! We'll decide later.

117
00:25:18,600 --> 00:25:32,940
You've been listening to Hikasa—

118
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:47,200
“Sakura Won't Allow It.” You're just making this up, Hikasa-san! You tried to make me read “Sakura,” but the script isn't marked that way. I won't know unless you tell me. You should be able to sense it from the atmosphere!

119
00:25:47,200 --> 00:25:49,740
Isn't it unusual to say your own name there?

120
00:25:50,140 --> 00:26:03,280
Shall we try it? Let's. Here we go: “You've been listening to Hikasa and Sakura—” Eek, embarrassing! What comes next? Who reads “Won't Allow Digressions”? We're approaching the ending...

121
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:05,260
You're doing whatever you like. Isn't this far too improvised?

122
00:26:05,700 --> 00:26:20,460
We welcome messages from our listeners. For postcards, write to: Bunka Hoso, “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions,” postal code 105-8002. For email, use all lowercase: yodan@joqr.net.

123
00:26:20,460 --> 00:26:37,960
That's Y-O-D-A-N at joqr.net. I already said it! You added that yourself. We're only on episode one and you're already rewriting the script too much. The program airs every Tuesday at 1:00 a.m. on terrestrial radio through Bunka Hoso.

124
00:26:37,960 --> 00:26:51,280
Every Thursday at 9:30 p.m., it is released with video; Internet radio station Onsen also releases an audio-only archive every Thursday at 10:00 p.m. And one more announcement: we're creating a YouTube channel for the show.

125
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:58,500
Or rather, it already exists. What? It does? Who made that without asking us? You did it without asking! Did you contact us?

126
00:26:58,920 --> 00:26:59,120
Did you?

127
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:03,520
Did you contact freelance Hikasa-san and Sakura-san from Aoni Production?

128
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:05,180
Did you? You really did?

129
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:19,320
Apparently they did. If you join the channel membership, you can watch the video archive and the after-talk. Only the first one is free! The after-talk is only free for the first episode too. Is that right?

130
00:27:19,380 --> 00:27:32,140
Maybe not—the after-talk may be free too. Please subscribe to the YouTube channel. Apparently memberships cannot start until we reach at least 1,000 subscribers and enough watch time, so everything will remain free until then.

131
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:35,120
Maybe we shouldn't read the next line.

132
00:27:35,860 --> 00:27:36,620
Should we say it quietly?

133
00:27:37,680 --> 00:27:50,180
In other words, if nobody watches, it stays free forever. But if nobody watches, you won't get to see us at slightly better quality. YouTube has better picture quality? Only slightly?

134
00:27:50,580 --> 00:28:03,960
Hire hair and makeup! Hire them! No, we won't. But it'll be clearer, won't it? Unless it looks pixelated like one of those horror films—

135
00:28:03,960 --> 00:28:18,560
like Paranormal Activity—we'll use up all our makeup. I'm fine. I don't want it because it takes too long. If my senior is fine with it, then so am I.

136
00:28:18,560 --> 00:28:31,800
Saku-chan has such beautiful skin she doesn't need makeup anyway. Hiyo-san, sorry, but let me warn you about one thing. You don't have to look down just because I pointed down—the audience only has audio here.

137
00:28:31,800 --> 00:28:47,200
Right. At some point I may suddenly start appearing with no makeup; sorry. On my previous radio show, there were days when I lost the energy to put it on partway through.

138
00:28:47,200 --> 00:29:03,360
Isn't that amazing? She walks into a video recording without makeup. We've digressed too much, so we'll end here tonight. “Hikasa and Sakura—”

139
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:19,160
“Won't Allow Digressions.” We read that part together! Okay. Hikasa reads here, Sakura reads there. This is the YouTube-exclusive after-talk for the program that airs every Tuesday at 1:00 a.m. on Bunka Hoso,

140
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:32,860
is released with video every Thursday at 9:30 p.m., and has an audio-only archive on Internet radio station Onsen every Thursday at 10:00 p.m.

141
00:29:32,860 --> 00:29:38,840
It's a bit late to say this, but the show's release format is far too complicated. What?

142
00:29:39,600 --> 00:29:51,920
You know those neat square charts that look like they were made in Excel—“audio airs at this time,” and so on? We should make one and pin it on X.

143
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:57,840
Make it cute. By the way, today's outfit of mine... What about it?

144
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:09,840
There was someone dressed like this among Howl's enemies, wasn't there? Yes, one of those flapping crow-like creatures. Hiyo-san, is this part on video?

145
00:30:09,840 --> 00:30:10,840
Is this on video?

146
00:30:10,840 --> 00:30:24,840
It is? Good—then people can see it. I can no longer tell which parts have video. I talk through all of the after-talk as though it is on video, which isn't good. Apparently the main program simply fades out somewhere while we're talking.

147
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:29,820
Scary! So they cut us off somewhere while we're doing our best to talk?

148
00:30:30,980 --> 00:30:46,580
We're hardly doing our best. It is a digression, after all. Even the picture has already cut away—we aren't on screen. This show is fun. It's hilarious that it may just continue forever.

149
00:30:46,580 --> 00:31:00,160
You're laughing. Incidentally, I was the one who invited you to do this show, Hiyo-san. That's right. And on that subject—

150
00:31:00,160 --> 00:31:13,240
when we were discussing whom I should do my next radio show with, Aoni Production president Kenjiro Takeuchi said,

151
00:31:13,240 --> 00:31:14,500
“What?”

152
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:19,080
“I'm a huge fan of Hikasa-san. How about doing a radio show with her?”

153
00:31:19,080 --> 00:31:23,320
Oh? The president of Aoni sounds rather rough, doesn't he?

154
00:31:24,660 --> 00:31:27,480
Soften it! No, no—was that really how he said it?

155
00:31:27,880 --> 00:31:45,900
His handshake is so forceful. “Aya-chan, good work! Sounds great—it'll be fun. Go for it!” Takeuchi-san apparently watched Hiyo-san for years when he worked at a certain Kansai radio station.

156
00:31:45,900 --> 00:31:46,400
What?

157
00:31:46,400 --> 00:32:00,940
He worked on Mr. Onosaka's show at a Kansai radio station and heard Hiyo-san talk there. He also listened to the radio show I did with Tone-san at the time.

158
00:32:00,940 --> 00:32:14,780
So he thought that if the two people from those old shows joined forces... Don't bring those programs up; both have ended! Fine, I understand. You mean—

159
00:32:14,780 --> 00:32:28,260
the man who wore enormous prayer beads around his wrist? Was that him? He definitely wore them back then. And every Aoni manager seemed to wear one of those Bluetooth headsets.

160
00:32:28,260 --> 00:32:42,440
Don't they all wear them? No? They're androids. Every Aoni manager is a cyborg.

161
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:49,440
They're Cyborg 009. But your current manager isn't wearing one, is he?

162
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:51,440
He's human—a fan of Seventeen. He certainly seems human.

163
00:32:52,440 --> 00:33:06,440
Very human, with real warmth. Maybe the women are usually human, while many of the men are cyborgs. They all have that seal—though perhaps I've only seen men wearing it here.

164
00:33:06,440 --> 00:33:10,440
Exactly. To prevent scandals, the company decided all the men should be androids.

165
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:25,960
What a terrifying company. I unexpectedly let things slip sometimes. Stop it—you keep leading me into them! I actually have sound judgment.

166
00:33:25,960 --> 00:33:26,580
Do you?

167
00:33:26,960 --> 00:33:40,180
When I join your guided tour, you point me this way, then that way, and—boom! I'm usually standing in the safe zone.

168
00:33:40,180 --> 00:33:53,880
Don't leave me alone to explode! Bunka Hoso is my home ground. In my early twenties, I hosted a live show here. Was it the “mamemme” one—Digista? You know it well.

169
00:33:53,880 --> 00:34:07,280
“Mamemme”? What's that? In a Digista commercial, Asuka Okame misread 真面目, “majime,” as “mamemme,” and they kept repeating it throughout the commercial. I vaguely remember that.

170
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:21,300
It ran during shows like Anispa, so I kept hearing “mamemme, mamemme.” I can no longer retrieve the rest of that memory.

171
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:23,320
See? That makes us sound like voice actors, doesn't it?

172
00:34:23,780 --> 00:34:38,200
Voice actors can't read kanji. Daisuke Namikawa is their leading example. Stop, stop! It's probably written on Wikipedia anyway. He can read them now—apparently he genuinely couldn't in the past.

173
00:34:38,200 --> 00:34:52,460
He's our senior! What was the story again? “Minna no doyōbi.” What's that? Are you sure we should say this when he isn't here?

174
00:34:52,460 --> 00:35:06,260
This is a trial in absentia. Namikawa-san once spectacularly misread a written phrase as “minna no doyōbi”—“everyone's Saturday.” It was a genius-level mistake.

175
00:35:06,260 --> 00:35:20,200
“Everyone's Saturday” sounds oddly idyllic and heartwarming: “Hooray, it's everyone's Saturday!”

176
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:33,480
Namikawa-san truly is a genius. And there was “remon na kemono”—“a lemon-like beast.” How do you even get “lemon”? I used to want to become like Namikawa-san.

177
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:44,440
You're speaking as though you haven't. You're probably the closest person to him. But I really can read kanji! Which is it?

178
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:58,300
“Mamemme.” Can you read them or not? I can! And I know how to look them up on an iPhone.

179
00:35:58,300 --> 00:36:00,080
You just draw the character. Are you okay?

180
00:36:00,260 --> 00:36:13,780
You're making fun of Namikawa-san far too much. He's amazing—being a company president is impressive. Perhaps even his inability to read is calculated.

181
00:36:13,780 --> 00:36:27,200
I sometimes think it may all be calculated. It seems so calculated that I think it probably isn't. So the outcome merely works that way?

182
00:36:27,200 --> 00:36:40,460
I think he's completely sincere. The other day I saw Namikawa-san wearing a very boldly patterned shirt, and the pattern was exactly the same as the paper bags from stationery shop Itoya.

183
00:36:40,460 --> 00:36:54,540
When I said, “You look like Itoya,” he replied, “Kimura Takuya has the same one.” Suddenly Kimutaku had entered the conversation. That was close—but invoking Kimutaku isn't a get-out-of-jail-free card.

184
00:36:54,540 --> 00:37:08,180
Exactly. He's everyone's idol, Kimura Takuya. That brings the after-talk to an end. Please enjoy the main program too. Your hosts were Yoko Hikasa and Ayane Sakura.

185
00:37:08,180 --> 00:37:17,720
Bye-bye! Wow, that's the first time I haven't done a proper “bye-bye” at the end of a voice-actor radio show. The first time?

186
00:37:18,040 --> 00:37:27,740
I always wave and say it like this. Something feels wrong. Go ahead, then. Bye-bye! Bye-bye! That's so nice—I'm jealous.
